  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 108 AM CST Sun Nov 22 2026\n\nWater vapor imagery at 06z depicts an upper trough axis extending \nfrom a closed low in Manitoba down through the Plains across western \nKS and into the OK/TX panhandles. Low-amplitude ridging follows over \nthe Intermountain West with the next closed low off the Pacific \nNorthwest coast. At the surface, the trough axis has moved through \nthe MO River Valley with the cold front almost entirely through the \nforecast area. Some post-frontal showers remain across the eastern \nhalf of the CWA, which should come to an end by sunrise as the best \nsynoptic forcing moves east of the area. With the upper trough axis \ndeparting east by midday, clouds should clear through the morning, \nallowing for a sunny end to the weekend. Strengthening CAA in the \nwake of the cold front brings a sharp contrast from the last several \ndays, however. Parts of east central KS may reach the low 50s, but \nmost places will struggle to reach that mark, only seeing highs in \nthe mid to upper 40s.\n\nCanadian high pressure builds into the area this evening and \novernight, allowing temperatures to plummet into the 20s under clear \nskies and light winds. While this looks favorable for radiational \ncooling, dew point depressions look wide enough at this time to \npreclude a widespread fog setup. That's not to say the low-lying \nvalley areas couldn't cool down enough to lead to some patchy fog \ngiven the recent rainfall, but this would be pretty localized with \nno real impacts anticipated if it occurs at all. \n\nA southern stream trough looks to pass through the Red River Valley \narea Tuesday with a northern stream system passing southeast through \nthe Northern Plains into the MO River Valley Wednesday. The track of \nthese waves don't really put our area in the best position for \nprecip anyway, but a lack of moisture also is expected to keep us \ndry. The air mass doesn't change in a meaningful way through \nThanksgiving, so temperatures stay below average through that time \nframe with highs in the 40s and lows in the 20s, perhaps a few \nteens. Tuesday looks like the coldest day of the week when 850mb \ntemps fall below 0C, limiting highs to the upper 30s to low 40s.\n\nBy Thursday night and Friday morning, the deterministic models show \nmore notable differences in the evolution of the next couple of \nshortwaves ejecting out of the Pacific trough. This would influence \nthe strength of the ridge over our area into next weekend, which can \nbe seen in the ensemble clusters as well. As such, the NBM does have \na warming trend Friday onward, closer to seasonal normals, but it \nremains to be seen how drastic of a warm-up it will be. In any case, \na lack of moisture keeps a dry forecast in place, allowing for good \ntravel conditions through the holiday weekend.",
